// The workspace `go` directive must be >= every module's, so it is a floor, normally set by the
// highest `go` line any workspace module declares. The floor was moved to 1.27.0
// together with the toolchain (below) as a deliberate step, ahead of any module needing it:
// every module still declares an older line, which the rule allows. Ask the tree rather
// than this comment: grep -H '^go ' $(go list -m -f '{{.GoMod}}').
//
// 🔴 THIS LINE MOVES WITH A DEPENDENCY BUMP, AND NOTHING BUMPS IT FOR YOU. A dependency
// that declares a newer language version raises the go directive of the MODULE that
// takes it, and Dependabot raises only that module's line and never looks at the
// workspace — so the module lands above this floor and the workspace then refuses to
// load AT ALL. Every Go module's CI job fails, including modules the bump never touched,
// with an error naming the one module that moved. The blast radius makes it read as a
// broad breakage; it is one line, here.
//
// No module or dependency version is named in the comment above deliberately: a number
// frozen in prose drifts away from the tree, and this one already had.
go 1.27.0

// 🔑 THE TOOLCHAIN IS PINNED SEPARATELY FROM THE LANGUAGE FLOOR, AND THEY MOVE FOR
// DIFFERENT REASONS. The `go` line above is a floor set by what our modules declare and
// it changes when a dependency raises it. This line is which compiler actually builds —
// it changes when a Go patch release fixes something we ship, which is a security
// cadence, not a dependency one. Conflating them is how a stdlib CVE fix gets blocked on
// an unrelated language-version argument.
//
// On the Go 1.27 line since 2026-10-13, at 1.27.3 or newer. 1.27.2 carried the fixes for
// the eleven reachable standard-library advisories published 2026-10-08 —
// GO-2026-6599, -6600, -6603, -6605, -6607, -6608, -6610, -6611, -6612, -6613 and
// -6617, across net/http (HTTP/1 and HTTP/2, client and server), html/template,
// crypto/tls and net/textproto / mime/multipart. 1.27.3 adds the fix for GO-2026-6809
// (crypto/x509: Certificate.Verify now rejects known-compromised intermediates).
// The toolchain, not the floor, is what carries the fixes.
//
// 🔴 THIS IS THE CHECK THAT GOES RED WITHOUT ANYONE TOUCHING THE CODE. govulncheck reads
// a LIVE advisory database, so the same commit that passed this morning fails this
// afternoon when an advisory lands. A red govulncheck on a PR therefore says nothing
// about that PR until you have checked whether main is red too — here every failing
// module was failing on code the PR never touched, including one byte-identical to main.
toolchain go1.27.3

use (
	./backend/admin/dc-broker-admin
	./backend/broker/dc-nats-server
	./backend/cli
	./backend/core
	./backend/edge/dc-edge-agent
	./backend/k8s
	./backend/services/ai-inference
	./backend/services/command-delivery
	./backend/services/dashboard-management
	./backend/services/device-management
	./backend/services/device-state
	./backend/services/event-management
	./backend/services/event-processing
	./backend/services/event-sources
	./backend/services/lwm2m-ingest
	./backend/services/mcp
	./backend/services/notification-management
	./backend/services/outbound-connectors
	./backend/services/sparkplug-ingest
	./backend/services/update-management
	./backend/services/user-management
	./backend/sims/dc-simulator
	./backend/tools/apiprobe
	./backend/tools/credguard
	./backend/tools/docsgql
	./backend/tools/drdrill
	./backend/tools/migrationdiff
	./backend/tools/muxguard
	./backend/tools/phaseguard
	./backend/tools/promqlguard
	./backend/tools/rdbguard
	./backend/tools/subconfirm
	./deploy
	./examples/ota-device
)
